{
  "note": "Everyday shop-floor words for careers, so a search for the word a plant uses finds the career in the catalogue. Each career lists the other names it goes by; a name points to one career only. Every title is a career of data/careers/catalogue.json. The titles a career used to be listed under are in the catalogue's `was` and are not repeated here. Searching reads these without regard to case, punctuation, '&' or a plural, and already finds a career by the words of its own title in any order (\"maint tech\" finds Maintenance Technician), so only names that differ from the title belong here. `not` lists names that are not this career's after all: a title the catalogue folded into it (`was`) or an everyday name once listed here that means other work at other employers. The migration deletes such a name from the career and says so in load_log, and the posting reader does not read it; a name is never deleted any other way. Work that is not manufacturing (warehouse, office, janitorial cleaning) has no career in the catalogue and is left out on purpose, and `notOurs` lists the words that make a posting's title such work whatever career's name stands beside them (the reader turns a title holding one away before it reads anything else; the builder's --check holds the list's shape and that no career is named with one of its words); so is cleaning a food plant and preparing food by hand (sanitation, meat cutting, slaughter, baking: the catalogue's `removed` says why), whose names are no career's: \"Oven Operator\", which the catalogue folded into the paint shop's career, is taken back from it because a bakery's oven operator carries the same title. Loaded by scripts/build-career-lists-sql.mjs. Migrations 0027 and 0047 were generated from earlier lists and are not regenerated (0056 was the second, 0074 the third; 0085 is the latest); a change here goes out as a new generated migration.",
